AWS have added a new Transaction ID field to their ALB logs, and have not announced this so it's not in their docs yet. However for those who are on the rollout plan, the `parse_aws_alb_log` function is failing:

```
Vector unable to parse alb log: function call error for "parse_aws_alb_log" at (18:45): Log should be fully consumed: " TID_XXXX"
```

The parse_aws_alb_log function should have added to it the ability to conditionally parse this entry, if that's possible, until AWS confirm the rollout is complete.

To workaround this I have added:

```
strip_tid, err = split(.message, " TID_")[0]
if err != null {
    .@error = true
    .@error_msg = "Vector unable to parse alb log: " + err
} else {
    structured, err = parse_aws_alb_log(strip_tid)
    if err != null {
        .@error = true
        .@error_msg = "Vector unable to parse alb log: " + err
    } else {
        . = merge(., structured)
    }
}
```

I spent a while tracking down why something wasn't working, and the problem was that I had misunderstood the `convert_to_string` function:

```
pub(crate) fn convert_to_string(value: Value, to_lowercase: bool) -> Result<String, ValueError> {
    let string = value.try_bytes_utf8_lossy()?;
    Ok(match to_lowercase {
        true => string.to_string(),
        false => string.to_lowercase(),
    })
}
```

The argument `to_lowercase` can be used to convert the string to be converted to lowercase, but the caller must set it to `false` to do this! I suggest renaming this argument to `is_case_sensitive` or something like that to prevent confusion about its meaning.

Our applications log in the following format:
[20230813_060934_933][W][TcpClient.cpp::Reconnect:861]Reconnect(id:687 ip:172.26.165.6 port:20882) failed(-17)[TID:118242]

The logstash dissect parsing is implemented as follows：
dissect {
		mapping => {
			"message" => "[%{time}][%{level}][%{file}::%{function}:%{line}]%{content}[TID:%{tid}]"
		}
	}

Does Vector have a parsing function similar to the Logstash Dissect filter plugin's parsing capability?
Dissect differs from Grok in that it does not use regular expressions and is faster. Dissect works well when data is reliably repeated.

I have seen some issues regarding this and they refer to `map_values`, `map_keys` and `redact` functions but all of them falls short on for this.

Let's assume we have a log entry as below and we would like to transform it to ensure data safety and regulations such as GDPR.
Here is input:
```json
{
  "user": {
    "email": "john@doe.com",
    "phone_number": "+1 (123) 232 11 11",
  },
  "event_details": {
     "provider": {
        "settings": {
           "api_key": "MY_SECRET_KEY",
           "api_url": "https://myapiurl.com"
        },
     }
  }
}
```

And here is an expected result:
```json
{
  "user": {
    "email": "j***@d**.com",
    "phone_number": "+1 (1**) *** ** 11",
  },
  "event_details": {
     "provider": {
        "settings": {
           "api_key": "HIDDEN",
           "api_url": "https://myapiurl.com"
        },
     }
  }
}
```

Currently only way to reach this result is by checking with `exists` and applying modification. Imagine you have lots of keys to be checked, our vrl script will be full of if checks.

But if we had a way to reach to path in `map_values` it would cover almost all cases. With this way we could provide lots of keys easily.

For ex:
```
to_be_redacted = [ "event_details.provider.settings.api_key" ]
to_be_masked = {
  "user.email": "regex_here"
}

map_values(.) -> |value, key, path| { 
  if( includes( to_be_redacted, path ) ) {
    "HIDDEN"
  } else if ( includes( keys(to_be_masked), path ) ) {
    r = to_regex!( get!(value: to_be_masked, path: split(path, "." ) ) )
    replace(value, r)
  } else {
    value
  }
}
```

Please consider this. Thanks

I am trying to check if http_slug starts with any of the known paths (it's an array)  then assign that known value to http_slug_known. However I am not able to "stop" processing/enumerating subsequent items in the loop once the if condition has become true. I tried break keyword but it said it's unimplemented yet. Any thoughts on how can I achieve this?

```
# List of known paths
known_paths = [
    "/admin","/forgot", "/reset", "/logout", "/api"
]

http_slug_known = "unknown"
for_each(known_paths) -> |index, path| {
    if starts_with(to_string(http_slug), path) {
        http_slug_known = path
    }
}
```


Current Workaround:

```
found = false
for_each(known_paths) -> |index, path| {
    if !found && starts_with(to_string(http_slug), path) {
        http_slug_known = path
        found = true
    }
}
```

Would be nicer to have a `break` keyword implemented.
